    "breadcrumb": "Core Design \u003e Threads",
    "content": "Working draft for faculty review. A spiral deepens through escalating passes across the two-year core. This is the spiral; one of eight spirals (with three lenses and one bounded practice — twelve threads total, plus two cross-cutting norms).\nIn one line: Understanding programs — your own and others’ — from ‘make it work’ to ‘defend it under scrutiny.’\nCharacter The lens shifts each pass rather than just raising difficulty: produce → understand others’ → organize → reason about → defend.\nPasses across the two years Course What’s new at this pass CIS 116 (Y1 Fall) — Introduction to Programming Write small correct programs across the paradigms it introduces (imperative and functional, with the same widgets carried forward from the original design); “make it work.” Also a first, informal comprehension touch in the same course — SLO 4 is explicitly “read, interpret, and modify existing computer programs,” ahead of CIS 200’s formal Code Archaeology pass. CIS 200 (Y1 Spring) — Programming Fundamentals Read others’ code, explain unfamiliar behavior — the thread’s signature “Code Archaeology” assessment; “understand before judging.” Encapsulation/abstraction as comprehension tools (tied to the course’s OOP introduction) and functional-pattern formalization (map/filter/reduce): organize so intent is legible. CIS 301 (Y2 Fall) — Logical Foundations of Programming Reason systematically about whether code does what it should — this course’s own confirmed purpose is deepening propositional/predicate logic into program verification, a natural extension of comprehension into formal reasoning. CIS 400 (Y2 Spring) — capstone of the core Defend your understanding and choices under scrutiny — a live walkthrough / architecture defense is the course’s actual assessment mechanism, not spec-compliance alone. Confirmed real design intent, not yet in the drafted course page (2026-08-03). Verified against resources/design-log.md — genuinely decided, not invented — but not yet reflected in cis-400.md’s own Topics/Schedule/Assignments, which are still generic/TBD. See the new “Confirmed Design Intent” section added to that page. Connections Code Archaeology (CIS 200) is the signature assessment this thread builds toward. Adjacent to Correctness \u0026 Verification (reasoning about code) and Professional Practices (documentation as explanation). CIS 141 applies this thread’s terminal “defend under scrutiny” skill to a narrower, specific case: critiquing AI-generated code for correctness and security, the confirmed prerequisite skill for the Year 3-4 Agentic AI specialization. Owned by the AI-Assisted Development practice (practice-ai-assisted.md) as its own terminal pass, not claimed here as a separate escalation step. Open questions for faculty review None outstanding; coherent in the current course mapping. For how this lands in a specific course, see that course’s design page under content/course-designs/.",
