    "breadcrumb": "Core Design \u003e Threads",
    "content": "Working draft for faculty review. A spiral deepens through escalating passes across the two-year core. This is the spiral; one of eight spirals (with three lenses and one bounded practice — twelve threads total, plus two cross-cutting norms).\nIn one line: Computing in service of human needs: design FOR, communicate TO, and validate WITH people — perceive → represent → comprehend → defend → fitness-for-purpose.\nCharacter Carries two embedded throughlines (accessibility + data visualization) and owns validation-with-users (acceptance testing, A/B testing), which is the “is it what they needed” question — distinct from the “is it correct” question that Correctness \u0026 Verification keeps.\nPasses across the two years Course What’s new at this pass CIS 120 (Y1 Fall) — Web Foundations Accessibility basics + visual hierarchy as human comprehension (PERCEIVE). CIS 140 (semester not yet confirmed in cs.md) — Foundations of Artificial Intelligence SLO 4 explicitly requires “introductory data analysis tools to explore and visualize datasets” — a real, confirmed visualization touchpoint, pending this course’s formal addition to the degree map (see open questions). CIS 320 (Y2 Fall) — User Experience Development Honest data visualization — represent data for humans (REPRESENT), on the client side of the CIS 300/320 pairing. Proposed addition, not yet in CIS 320’s drafted content — see the “Proposed Changes” section of cis-320.md. CIS 260 (Y1 Spring) — Foundations of Relational Databases SQL as an investigation tool — writing queries to find, filter, and summarize real-world data to answer questions (COMPREHEND). Not a visualization touchpoint; the course’s actual content is query-writing only. CIS 320 (Y2 Fall), continued Formal, bounded exposure to acceptance-criteria and A/B-testing process work (FITNESS) — every student gets this once. cs.md is explicit that a full acceptance-testing deep dive is deliberately deferred to a Software Architecture specialization elective instead, not duplicated here. CIS 400 (Y2 Spring) — capstone of the core Defend human-facing choices under scrutiny — applying, not introducing, the acceptance-criteria concept CIS 320 already gave every student. Connections Data-viz throughline converges with the Trustworthy Computing lens (honest charts). CIS 400’s defend pass sits beside Correctness \u0026 Verification — two kinds of “does it work” (fitness vs correctness) converge but answer different questions. Open questions for faculty review Data visualization is embedded, never a course slot — confirm faculty can see/assess it. Confirm the two throughlines (accessibility + data viz) read clearly as one thread. CIS 320 density, sharpened (2026-08-03). It now carries the data-visualization (REPRESENT, proposed) and acceptance-testing (FITNESS, confirmed) passes on a 1-credit course — worth watching alongside the program’s other density flags (CIS 115, CIS 260, CIS 300), though smaller in scale than those. The visualization content is still only proposed, not drafted, so this will get more concrete once cis-320.md is fleshed out. CIS 140 and CIS 142 are confirmed real, separate courses (2026-08-03) but are missing from cs.md entirely — unlike CIS 141 and CIS 251, which are both confirmed there. Neither has a semester, credit-hour, or prerequisite entry in the degree map yet. This thread’s CIS 140 pass can’t be placed with real confidence in the two-year sequence until that’s resolved — flagged as a needed follow-up for whoever owns cs.md (course-designer/packaging-mapper territory), not something to guess here. For how this lands in a specific course, see that course’s design page under content/course-designs/.",
    "description": "Working draft for faculty review. A spiral deepens through escalating passes across the two-year core. This is the spiral; one of eight spirals (with three lenses and one bounded practice — twelve threads total, plus two cross-cutting norms).\nIn one line: Computing in service of human needs: design FOR, communicate TO, and validate WITH people — perceive → represent → comprehend → defend → fitness-for-purpose.\nCharacter Carries two embedded throughlines (accessibility + data visualization) and owns validation-with-users (acceptance testing, A/B testing), which is the “is it what they needed” question — distinct from the “is it correct” question that Correctness \u0026 Verification keeps.",
