    "breadcrumb": "Pedagogical Approach \u003e Signature Applications",
    "content": "Working draft for faculty review. This page describes a real institutional asset, not a hypothetical example — see Signature Applications for why the program prefers real assets over invented ones.\nWhat it is “Where Did They Go?” (repo: github.com/ksu-cs/development-project-ksds) is an active external project — Vue + JavaScript + D3.js frontend, Node.js backend, static JSON/GeoJSON data, MIT license, 12+ releases as of mid-2026. It’s a partnership between K-State CS and the Chapman Center for Rural Studies (History department), visualizing rural population migration in Kansas — county borders, rail/interstate infrastructure, Army Corps of Engineers lakes, town populations, hospitals, schools — with playback over time.\nBeing built for eventual multi-state reuse, not Kansas-only (confirmed 2026-07-15) — worth keeping in mind before treating any Kansas-specific framing as permanent.\nThis is a consumption/context asset, not a student build target. Per the confirmed CIS 400 capstone design, student teams do not modify this codebase directly — it’s a live external project with its own release cadence and non-CS stakeholders (Chapman Center historians). It’s used as motivating context and a data-consumption source, not something student teams ship changes to.\nWhat’s actually real right now Capability Status First curricular need Static GeoJSON/JSON consumption requested Data Science track, sequence point TBD That’s the entire confirmed/requested surface — a batch/generated-data consumption pattern, a real contrast to the plant-sensor API’s live-request pattern.\nRelationship to the historical archive “Where Did They Go?” consumes static files generated by a separate, not-yet-built system — the historical archive — which is where the community-facing correction/versioning/search work (and CIS 400’s actual capstone project) lives. Treat the two as coupled but distinct: this page’s asset is the existing, external, read-mostly visualization; the historical archive is the green-field system student teams help build.\nOpen questions for faculty review No course/sequence point confirmed yet — flagged as “TBD” in the source roadmap itself, not a gap introduced here. As an external, actively-developed project, format or endpoint changes are a real risk to assignment stability — the embedding-systems teaching point (“what if CS’s assumptions about this data silently break”) is itself pedagogically useful, but any assignment depending on it needs a stability check before delivery. See resources/reference/ks-population-trends-roadmap.md for the live capability-tracking table (shared with the historical archive).",
    "description": "Working draft for faculty review. This page describes a real institutional asset, not a hypothetical example — see Signature Applications for why the program prefers real assets over invented ones.\nWhat it is “Where Did They Go?” (repo: github.com/ksu-cs/development-project-ksds) is an active external project — Vue + JavaScript + D3.js frontend, Node.js backend, static JSON/GeoJSON data, MIT license, 12+ releases as of mid-2026. It’s a partnership between K-State CS and the Chapman Center for Rural Studies (History department), visualizing rural population migration in Kansas — county borders, rail/interstate infrastructure, Army Corps of Engineers lakes, town populations, hospitals, schools — with playback over time.",
