    "content": "Course Description A study of the ethical issues raised by computing technologies and the impact on society. Exploration of how one might justify actions in regard to ethical dilemmas within the fields of technology, computer science, information security, and artificial intelligence. Other topics include an examination of terminology used, Professional Codes of Conduct for computer science professionals, ethics of software development, and ethical issues relating to privacy and intellectual property in cyberspace.\nCredit Hours 3 credits (current catalog). Redesign note: resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md flags this “may reduce to 1 [credit] as ethics is integrated into the core” — not yet decided.\nCourse Overview Not yet drafted — pending course-designer’s detailed pass. The Trustworthy Computing lens (content/core-design/lenses/lens-trustworthy-computing.md) is the core’s primary ethics throughline, formalized in CIS 251 — this course’s future role alongside that lens is not yet settled.\nCourse Goals and Learning Outcomes Not yet drafted — pending course-designer’s detailed pass.\nCourse Prerequisites Prerequisite: CIS 300 (current catalog).",
